description.narrative:
The overall objective of the project is to contribute to the sustainable management of biodiversity rich natural forests of the Southwest Ethiopia for enhancing ecosystem services and livelihoods improvement of communities living in and around the forest landscapes in Southwest Ethiopia. Furthermore, the project will contribute towards several commitments of Ethiopia for global partnerships such as the Paris Agreement of the UNFCCC, land degradation neutrality of the UNCCD, conservation targets and ambitions of the UN Convention on Biological Diversity (CBD), AF100, AUs Agenda 2063 and the SDG. 

The specific objectives of the project are:
1To strengthen mainstreaming and integration of biodiversity conservation interventions into local development programs;
2To enhance the sustainable forest management in selected woredas and zones in Oromia and SWEP Regional States;
3To improve gender equality and livelihoods of forest dependent communities, including women and youth; and
4To build the capacity of Government stakeholders and local community groups at various levels.

Several results are reported under 2025 implementation (p. 5-35 of the Narrative Report).

The following is a summary of results for each of the 4 outcomes and at "systems-change" levels.

A. Key results per outcome area

i) Outcome 1 (biodiversity conservation): The project secured 24 ecologically critical pilot sites covering about 955 hectares (including about 68 ha of wetlands) through integration into formal land-use plans, strengthening long-term conservation and ecological resilience.

ii) Outcome 2 (forest management): Community-based systems were significantly strengthened, with 640 ha of natural forest brought under participatory forest management (PFM) and formally managed by local cooperatives, improving sustainable governance and stewardship.  

iii) Outcome 3 (gender equality and livelihoods): The project enabled close to 2,360 livelihood beneficiaries (56% women) to engage in diversified, market-oriented income activities, demonstrating strong gender inclusion and economic impact.

iv) Outcome 4 (institutional capacity and coordination): Multi-stakeholder coordination was enhanced through seven steering committee meetings involving at least 1,080 participants (about 210 women), strengthening collaboration, accountability, and implementation capacity across sectors.

B. Changes of practice taking hold

Keeping in mind that CSMFL is more of a direct-delivery project (not an MSD program or similar), the following changes of practice can be reported as taking shape by the end of the 3rd year period.

i) Integrated Land-use Planning (ILUP) institutionalized at local government and community levels;

ii) Participatory Forest Management (PFM) formalized as a recognized forest governance system;

iii) Gender-responsive leadership normalized in both government and communities;

iv) Shift toward climate-smart, diversified, market-oriented agriculture;

v)  Village Economic and Social Associations (VESAs) functioning as sustainable rural financial systems; regular multi-stakeholder coordination established;

vi) Community-based renewable energy capacity embedded locally; and

vii) Restoration and nursery management becoming ongoing community systems.
